WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.880
C developers should rely on well-maintained cryptographic libraries and reviewed platform capabilities.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.540
Cryptographic design is specialized, and small mistakes can change the security meaning of a system.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:22.240
A maintained library gives the application reviewed implementations, safer API patterns, documentation, update paths, and behavior that is already tested across platforms.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.340
Avoid custom algorithms and hand-built protocols.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.720
A project may have unique application requirements, but that does not mean it needs unique cryptographic primitives.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:40.260
The safer approach is to select an organization-approved library, follow current documentation, and keep integration code small enough to review.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:51.680
Library choice should consider maintenance, documentation quality, supported platforms, secure defaults, update cadence, compliance requirements where relevant, and operational fit.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:01:00.300
If a project must meet a regulatory or procurement requirement, that requirement should be included in the library decision early rather than retrofitted after implementation.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.480
API contracts matter. Some libraries require explicit initialization and cleanup.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.260
Some return detailed verification results.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:16.480
Some expose one-shot APIs, streaming APIs, context objects, reference-counted objects, or platform-backed key stores.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:25.340
Developers should understand memory ownership, thread-safety expectations, error reporting, and cleanup rules before writing application logic around the library.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.040
Using a library does not remove responsibility.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:37.620
Teams still need to load keys correctly, choose approved settings, validate identities, check return values, test negative cases, and update dependencies.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.440
Safe cryptography in C is mostly the disciplined use of trusted APIs, not clever code.
